photographer Jonathan Albury travels the  world to document the stark realities of  war he has covered conflicts in places  from Afghanistan and Ethiopia to Ukraine  and Egypt but on a trip to Syria in  April of 2013 al Peru was kidnapped  while moving toward the frontlines his  new book details that 81 day ordeal the  shattered lens a war photographers true  story of captivity and survival in Syria  is published by atria an imprint of  Simon &amp; Schuster a division of CBS  Jonathan Alpert joins us now good  morning Jonathan morning take us back to  that moment when you were captain so  this was my third trip into Syria and  this time I decided to go through  Lebanon in order to cover the fighting  near Damascus which was very heavy at  the time and at the time the rebels were  still at the upper hands and there was  an anklet which you get smuggled into  and from there I cover the basically the  battle between mostly Hezbollah infantry  and syrian air force against various  rebel forces and after ten days the kids  side to kidnap me instead of letting me  go  do you remember what went through your  mind in that moment when you were  kidnapped yeah it's very strange I think  like most traumatic experiences  everything goes very slowly excuse me  and then you you think it's not really  happening and it's not real there's a  mistake and everything will get resolved  so usually there's that moment when you  you think that you're actually dreaming  what you're going through you were  blindfolded handcuffed and I know beaten  during your captivity 81 days yeah for  81 days the first month is love  torturing going on mock executions  interrogations and they play a game with  you where one day they're very nice and  the other day they're very difficult  with you I think it's meant partially to  break your will and to keep you on your  toes so you don't really know what to do  if you want to escape or try anything  against them so they really break you up  like that who were the captors so you  have as you know many rebel factions in  Syria it's a very complicated affair but  at the time I was mostly local militias  or fighting the regime but because they  were having such difficult problems with  the Hezbollah in French  they brought in a lot of Al nusra front  people were much more experienced and  discipline at fighting so they did it do  you think yeah I mean it's all connected  and yeah definitely you you talk in the  book about gaining the trust of your  captors how did you do that it's so nice  it's a question of survival you have to  pick out who you can trust and who you  don't trust usually I would go after the  younger soldiers who were more  interested in the fight I was from the  western world they were more curious  about me so they were nicer to me the  torturing and beatings were done mostly  by the older or the officer group so I  spent a lot of time manipulating these  young guys to make sure I would get the  Oh favors like making sure my handcuffs  were not as tight or maybe a lot to go  to the bathroom one extra time during  the day just very little things that  make your life easier so how did you  escape well there was a ransom which is  often the case when it comes to  kidnapping that was given out to by men  which I can't give the name of of course  but he he was part of a blacklist that  was written down by the European Union  and the United States of powerful Syrian  men like himself who are parliamentary  and close to the regime but also do  businesses around the world and when  you're on that list you can't travel  they freeze your assets and he wanted  that to be gone obviously so by paying  for me and releasing me he was hoping to  give to get a favor from the United  States or friends and be freed from it  which happened briefly and then he was  put back right on that list I would  would you ever go back to Syria of  course I always tempted to go back to  these different war zones just for  personal reasons after the war when it  ends which will most likely happen  fairly soon I would definitely like to  go back and see the places where I was  kid I was captured yeah well Jonathan  Albury thank you so much for joining us  and cue we appreciate it we're glad  you're back home safe
 

